{
  "platform_id": "0H3XfsdrXc0",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:0H3XfsdrXc0",
    "purpose": "A deep dive into the Feit Classic Low sneaker: its materials, tanning, horsehide leather, hand construction and price. It also explains why modern sneakers can't recreate the warmth of vintage ones. This is the first episode of the creator's 'World's Most Fantastic' series.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "The Feit Classic Low is a hand-lasted, hand-sewn sneaker made from vegetable-tanned horsehide and cork. Its handmade process and high-quality materials give it the warmth of vintage sneakers, which machine-made modern shoes lose.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "Feit Classic Lows are made mainly of cork and leather, plus a lot of glue, waxed cotton thread and Vibram pads.",
      "The leather is vegetable tanned rather than chrome tanned, which affects breathability, water resistance, durability, creaking and patina.",
      "The uppers are horsehide, which is denser than cowhide, so a thinner leather can break in quickly and still be strong.",
      "The shoes are hand lasted and hand sewn to the insole's gemming, not Goodyear welted. A leather strip hides the construction, and glue is used heavily to keep the look minimal.",
      "They retail at $850 and are made in a single factory in China. According to the founder, making the exact same shoe in Italy would cost about $2,500.",
      "Vintage sneakers had high-quality materials and hands-on processes, such as hand lasting, which give a warmth that machine-made modern sneakers lose."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to Feit",
        "summary": "Unboxing details and a hook about the single factory that makes the shoes. Vintage-feeling sneakers are introduced, along with the episode's questions about price and construction and the launch of the 'World's Most Fantastic' series.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 1
      },
      {
        "title": "Quality and aging process",
        "summary": "How the sneakers age over two months of wear, plus a disclaimer that he bought them himself and contacted the founder only to confirm facts.",
        "first_segment": 2,
        "last_segment": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Materials and tanning",
        "summary": "Cork as the base material and the Italian tannery that supplies the leather. Covers vegetable versus chrome tanning and their pros and cons, plus the creaking and patina of vegetable-tanned leather.",
        "first_segment": 3,
        "last_segment": 5
      },
      {
        "title": "Watch brand sponsorship",
        "summary": "Sponsor segment listing six facts about the Vaer R1 Chronograph watch.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 6
      },
      {
        "title": "Horsehide leather benefits",
        "summary": "Why horsehide is used: it is denser than cowhide, breaks in quickly while staying strong, and is metal-free tanned so it can be worn without socks.",
        "first_segment": 6,
        "last_segment": 7
      },
      {
        "title": "Handmade construction",
        "summary": "Explains lasting, hand versus machine lasting, and stitching to the insole gemming versus Goodyear welt construction. Covers the cork fill, the hiding leather strip, the leather outsole, the Vibram pads and the heavy use of glue.",
        "first_segment": 7,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "Pricing and manufacturing",
        "summary": "Why the shoes cost $850, the single factory in China, and the founder's claim that Italian production would cost $2,500. Includes his research comparing prices and US manufacturing.",
        "first_segment": 10,
        "last_segment": 12
      },
      {
        "title": "Modern vs vintage shoes",
        "summary": "Why vintage sneakers, cleats and running shoes look different from modern ones and can't be recreated: better early materials, hands-on processes such as hand lasting, and how big brands compensate for machine lasting.",
        "first_segment": 12,
        "last_segment": 15
      }
    ],
    "passages": [
